WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:07.680
For years, the React Native Bridge operated on a very specific bottlenecked system, loosely-typed,

2
00:00:07.680 --> 00:00:12.800
asynchronous message passing. Here's what that mechanical overhead looks like in practice.

3
00:00:13.360 --> 00:00:19.440
When you call a function, the arguments are serialized into a JSON string on the JavaScript thread,

4
00:00:19.440 --> 00:00:25.280
and then parsed back into a native object on the Android thread. That double conversion process

5
00:00:25.280 --> 00:00:32.160
introduces an unavoidable processing delay. As this diagram updates, we see the replacement mechanism.

6
00:00:32.160 --> 00:00:38.640
The old React context-based Java module is deprecated because it simply cannot execute synchronously

7
00:00:38.640 --> 00:00:44.800
or guarantee type safety. The new architecture strips out the JSON serialization entirely,

8
00:00:44.800 --> 00:00:50.880
replacing it with a direct memory pointer, powered by JSI, that connects the JavaScript payload

9
00:00:50.880 --> 00:00:57.280
directly to a C++ reference. Because this direct memory access happens at the C++ level,

10
00:00:57.280 --> 00:01:03.600
the old Java-based class structure is no longer sufficient. JSI needs a module structure it can see

11
00:01:03.600 --> 00:01:09.840
and interact with directly. This necessitates a shift to turbo modules, native modules that are built

12
00:01:09.840 --> 00:01:15.920
on top of a C++ layer to enable synchronous execution. Achieving this level of communication

13
00:01:15.920 --> 00:01:21.920
means abandoning dynamic, untyped JSON payloads. Everything must now adhere to a strictly defined,

14
00:01:21.920 --> 00:01:27.280
pre-compiled architectural contract. Moving to turbo modules means Android developers have to

15
00:01:27.280 --> 00:01:32.640
unlearn how native modules are structured and initialized. Let's look at this comparison table.

16
00:01:32.640 --> 00:01:37.200
Under the old system, every single native module was initialized at App Startup,

17
00:01:37.200 --> 00:01:43.040
dragging down load times. Turbo modules, however, are strictly lazy loaded. They initialize exactly

18
00:01:43.040 --> 00:01:48.800
when JavaScript requests them and not a millisecond sooner. The way we define methods changes too.

19
00:01:48.800 --> 00:01:54.400
You no longer scatter at react method annotations throughout your Java files. Instead, every method is

20
00:01:54.400 --> 00:02:00.000
defined strictly up front in a single TypeScript specification file. Structurally, you are no longer

21
00:02:00.000 --> 00:02:05.440
extending a base Java class. You write a Coplin class that implements a strictly typed interface

22
00:02:05.440 --> 00:02:11.600
generated by the build system. This shifts the entire burden of stability. Instead of relying on runtime

23
00:02:11.600 --> 00:02:16.560
error checking to catch mismatches, we lock in Type Safety at build time through code generation.

24
00:02:17.280 --> 00:02:22.000
In the new architecture, JavaScript dictates the structure. The native side is simply there to

25
00:02:22.000 --> 00:02:27.200
fulfill it. The binding contract between these two environments is the TypeScript spec files.

26
00:02:27.200 --> 00:02:32.400
This document tells the code generator exactly what C++ and Kotlin files it needs to build.

27
00:02:32.960 --> 00:02:37.920
To keep the bridge seamless, the code generator intentionally supports a highly restricted set of

28
00:02:37.920 --> 00:02:43.680
data types. In this mapping diagram, primitives cross cleanly. A TypeScript string or Boolean

29
00:02:43.680 --> 00:02:49.680
translates exactly. A TypeScript number maps specifically to a Kotlin double. For complex data,

30
00:02:49.680 --> 00:02:55.200
plain objects translate to readable map, arrays to readable array, and a promise maps directly to the

31
00:02:55.200 --> 00:03:01.360
bridge promise interface. These constraints are rigid by design. Every defined type must map across

32
00:03:01.360 --> 00:03:06.800
the TypeScript C++ and Kotlin boundaries without relying on slow runtime reflection.

33
00:03:07.520 --> 00:03:12.080
The moment you run your application build, an automated pipeline kicks in, triggered by the

34
00:03:12.080 --> 00:03:18.320
spec location registered in your package.json. The build script passes your TypeScript spec directly

35
00:03:18.320 --> 00:03:24.160
into the Cogen engine. As shown in this flow chart, the spec enters Cogen. The engine automatically

36
00:03:24.160 --> 00:03:30.000
writes all required C++ files and specific JNI glue for the JavaScript engine to speak to the Java

37
00:03:30.000 --> 00:03:36.400
virtual machine. Following the second branch, Cogen also outputs a fully typed Kotlin abstract class.

38
00:03:36.960 --> 00:03:41.680
From here, the developer's only job is to create a Kotlin class that inherits from this generated

39
00:03:41.680 --> 00:03:46.800
interface and implements the required logic. By handling the translation layer mechanically,

40
00:03:46.800 --> 00:03:51.920
Cogen eliminates the tedious manual boilerplate, freeing engineers to focus strictly on their native

41
00:03:51.920 --> 00:03:58.080
business logic. The standard promise-based API works, but promises lack built-in cancellation

42
00:03:58.080 --> 00:04:04.000
mechanisms if a react component un-mounts before the native work finishes. Because turbo modules

43
00:04:04.080 --> 00:04:09.920
load on demand and persist independently of individual UI screens, treating their background tasks

44
00:04:09.920 --> 00:04:14.400
like standard asynchronous Java processes creates a risk for concurrency issues.

45
00:04:14.960 --> 00:04:19.120
The most frequent mistake Android developers make here is spawning an anonymous

46
00:04:19.120 --> 00:04:23.520
co-routine scope inside a turbo-module method whenever they need to run a background thread.

47
00:04:24.080 --> 00:04:28.640
Take a look at this timeline. If the react component un-mounts and disappears,

48
00:04:28.640 --> 00:04:34.640
that anonymous co-routine has no life cycle awareness. The process thread continues executing in

49
00:04:34.640 --> 00:04:40.800
the background, outliving the context and creating a zombie process that leaks memory. The correct

50
00:04:40.800 --> 00:04:46.560
approach is structured concurrency. If you write Android apps, you can follow the same logic used

51
00:04:46.560 --> 00:04:53.200
in a V-model scope. The pattern in a turbo-module is to declare a single centralized module scope

52
00:04:53.200 --> 00:04:59.280
that is tied directly to the module's own life cycle. Turbo modules provide an invalidate method.

53
00:04:59.280 --> 00:05:04.080
This is the exact moment the react native framework signals that the module is being torn down.

54
00:05:04.640 --> 00:05:10.160
Watch what happens now. By calling modulescope.cancel inside the invalidate block,

55
00:05:10.160 --> 00:05:15.600
the disappearing react component effectively sends a termination signal, instantly and safely

56
00:05:15.600 --> 00:05:20.800
killing all running background co-routines. You must also select the correct dispatcher for that

57
00:05:20.800 --> 00:05:27.520
scope. Dispatchers.io provides a thread pool scaled specifically for blocking I.O. like network requests

58
00:05:27.520 --> 00:05:34.640
or database reads. In contrast, dispatchers.default limits its thread pool to the member of CPU course,

59
00:05:34.640 --> 00:05:40.800
making it ideal for intensive calculations without blocking. And dispatchers.main is strictly reserved

60
00:05:40.800 --> 00:05:47.040
for interacting with Android system APIs that mandate main thread access. Native background work

61
00:05:47.040 --> 00:05:51.600
must mirror the life cycle of the JavaScript environment. If the two fall out of sync,

62
00:05:51.600 --> 00:05:57.520
the native side may attempt to resolve data into a context that no longer exists. Promises solve

63
00:05:57.520 --> 00:06:03.200
single response tasks, but they resolve exactly once. If you build a turbo module for a Bluetooth

64
00:06:03.200 --> 00:06:09.440
scanner or a gyvoscope, a promise is useless. Android system APIs broadcast continuous data streams

65
00:06:09.440 --> 00:06:14.320
using ongoing callback loops. To bridge these continuous streams into the new architecture,

66
00:06:14.320 --> 00:06:20.400
we rely on a Kotlin feature called callbackflow. Callbackflow acts as a structural funnel. It captures

67
00:06:20.400 --> 00:06:25.520
every individual callback from the Android system and organizes them, emitting each sequentially onto

68
00:06:25.520 --> 00:06:32.080
a Kotlin flow. In this flow diagram, you can see the next step. Once the data leaves callback flow,

69
00:06:32.080 --> 00:06:37.680
the react native eventometer takes over, pushing those organized sequential blocks straight across

70
00:06:37.680 --> 00:06:43.520
the JSI boundary. Over in JavaScript, the application simply subscribes to those emissions using a

71
00:06:43.520 --> 00:06:49.360
standard event listener. By wrapping system callbacks in a flow, we replace fragile custom bridging

72
00:06:49.360 --> 00:06:55.840
logic with idiomatic Kotlin concurrency that plays with the JSI. Code Gen and Kotlin have all the vast

73
00:06:55.840 --> 00:07:01.440
majority of native use cases, but there are scenarios that require moving beyond the generated layer.

74
00:07:01.440 --> 00:07:06.400
The first scenario is when you need to wrap a pre-existing low-level C++ library,

75
00:07:06.400 --> 00:07:12.160
like an audio codec or OpenCv, where passing data back and forth through JNI would add unnecessary

76
00:07:12.160 --> 00:07:17.200
complexity. The second scenario is when you require the highest possible synchronous throughput,

77
00:07:17.200 --> 00:07:22.320
such as processing high-resolution camera frames, where every millisecond of JNI overhead matters.

78
00:07:22.880 --> 00:07:29.440
This decision tree illustrates the split. If you hit that second node, needing C++ integration

79
00:07:29.440 --> 00:07:37.920
or minimal latency, you bypass code Gen. Instead, you wire up a custom CMake lists.kxt build configuration.

80
00:07:38.400 --> 00:07:45.200
You then write a custom C++ class that inherits from host object. You manually construct the methods

81
00:07:45.200 --> 00:07:50.240
and install that object directly into the JavaScript runtimes memory space from the native side.

82
00:07:50.800 --> 00:07:57.280
Dropping down to raw C++ means trading away the automated safety and generated tests of Kotlin.

83
00:07:57.280 --> 00:08:03.760
In return, you gain direct access to the JS engine with the same performance profile as native C++ code.

84
00:08:03.840 --> 00:08:08.400
To build effectively in the new architecture, you just have to respect the routing.

85
00:08:08.400 --> 00:08:13.760
The TypeScript spec enforces your data contract, and code Gen automates the C++ glue.

86
00:08:14.240 --> 00:08:19.520
For standard APIs, your generated Kotlin interfaces must implement structured concurrency,

87
00:08:19.520 --> 00:08:23.600
using module scope cancellation to terminate tasks when the UI unmounts.

88
00:08:24.160 --> 00:08:29.200
Looking at this full architectural blueprint, we clearly see the exception path.

89
00:08:29.280 --> 00:08:36.880
Path B bypasses code Gen entirely, dropping directly to a C++ host object, reserved for C++ library

90
00:08:36.880 --> 00:08:42.720
wrappers or high throughput data processing. This ecosystem requires Android engineers to think in

91
00:08:42.720 --> 00:08:47.360
terms of memory ownership and lifecycle alignment, rather than simple message passing.

92
00:08:48.000 --> 00:08:53.040
By replacing the asynchronous bridge with direct memory sharing, the new architecture removes

93
00:08:53.040 --> 00:08:58.560
the software induced latency of the legacy system. The result is a more predictable foundation

94
00:08:58.560 --> 00:09:06.720
for building complex native features.

